      "transcript": "Mara Fenwick: Thanks for joining the Foxton volunteer morning. Keep the lock-flight plan in view while I identify safe routes, work points and team duties.\nMara Fenwick: For the tool store, use the small shed immediately west of the middle lock pound, reached from the towpath before the footbridge.\nMara Fenwick: Upstream is at the top of the map, and the numbered chambers step downhill towards the southern entrance.\nMara Fenwick: Wildlife screens protect a nesting strip near the reeds. Volunteers inspect from the towpath and photograph damage instead of stepping behind the barrier.\nMara Fenwick: The upper gate work area is beyond the footbridge at its north-eastern end, inside the fenced strip beside the top gate.\nMara Fenwick: The central towpath remains open to walkers, so equipment must stay inside the coned work areas rather than beside the water.\nMara Fenwick: A first-aid kit and drinking water remain at the tent, whereas personal bags belong in the staffed rest area to keep the route unobstructed.\nMara Fenwick: The spillway viewpoint stands at the far western edge, where the short gravel spur ends beside the overflow channel.\nMara Fenwick: Nobody crosses a gate beam, even when a chamber is empty; volunteers use the signed footbridge between the two paths.\nMara Fenwick: In heavy rain the painting task is cancelled, and that team joins the indoor equipment audit rather than waiting beside the exposed gates.\nMara Fenwick: The sign-in tent is just inside the south entrance, between the gate and the first bend in the towpath, not beside the café.\nMara Fenwick: The café veranda looks convenient for tools, but it is a public area and must remain clear throughout the morning.\nMara Fenwick: The morning begins with a toolbox check, since borrowed scrapers and brushes must be counted both when issued and when returned.\nMara Fenwick: The volunteer rest area occupies the grassy rectangle east of the third chamber, opposite the middle pound.\nMara Fenwick: If a boat approaches, the navigation crew controls the gates while volunteers step back behind the painted line.\nMara Fenwick: Canal staff, not volunteers, operate paddles or gate mechanisms; even experienced boaters in the group follow that restriction.\nMara Fenwick: The lower chamber is the first broad basin reached when you enter from the south and follow the towpath uphill.\nMara Fenwick: At midday all teams return borrowed brushes and grabbers to the store, then sign out at the same tent used on arrival.\nMara Fenwick: The nearest public toilets are beyond the café, while the staff washroom beside the store is not available to visitors.\nMara Fenwick: Team E begins with litter removal along the lower towpath, working uphill in pairs.\nMara Fenwick: The lock flight has ten chambers, but volunteer work is confined to the lower six because maintenance crews are operating farther uphill.\nMara Fenwick: Volunteers working near water wear the supplied buoyancy aids and remain with their allocated partner until reassigned by a coordinator.\nMara Fenwick: The safety-rail painting is assigned to Team G, after surfaces have dried.\nMara Fenwick: Notice the difference between the overflow channel and navigable water: the spillway may look shallow, yet its stone surface is slippery and out of bounds.\nMara Fenwick: Any oil sheen, dead fish or damaged masonry is reported by radio with a location; nobody collects a sample independently.\nMara Fenwick: Team B records visitor numbers at the crossing rather than at the entrance.\nMara Fenwick: Paint teams use water-based coating only on prepared rails; they do not paint timber gates, paddle gear or historic metal plates.\nMara Fenwick: The final sweep starts at the upper work area and moves downhill, preventing cleaned sections from being crossed repeatedly by returning teams.\nMara Fenwick: Team D checks the temporary wildlife screens and reports gaps without moving the stakes.\nMara Fenwick: The visitor-count sheet separates walkers, cyclists and boat crews so the charity can plan future signs without treating every passing person alike.\nMara Fenwick: That completes the lock-flight allocation. I will check every team at midday before tools return downhill to the store.",
