      "instructions": "31-36: Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.\n37-40: Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.",
      "transcript": "Dr Rowan Leith: Today I will walk you through a teaching simulation from our methods course. The measurements belong to the exercise, so concentrate on how the flock model behaves.\nDr Rowan Leith: A useful model begins with nearest neighbours: each bird responds to a limited social neighbourhood, not to every individual in the flock.\nDr Rowan Leith: Murmurations vary greatly in size, duration and setting, so one evening’s display cannot stand for every flock.\nDr Rowan Leith: A signal to turn is not transmitted like a command through a queue; each bird responds to changing motion in its own local field.\nDr Rowan Leith: To see why validation matters, imagine that tracking software swaps two bird identities; the reconstructed path would show a false acceleration that no bird actually made.\nDr Rowan Leith: When many birds repeatedly follow local rules, an organised group pattern can emerge without a leader.\nDr Rowan Leith: The appearance of central control is deceptive because repeated neighbour-to-neighbour adjustment can coordinate a much larger pattern.\nDr Rowan Leith: A published simulation in the course pack reports that flocks may become denser under predator pressure, strengthening the confusion effect in its results.\nDr Rowan Leith: A bird balances alignment with neighbours, attraction towards the group and avoidance of collision; changing those priorities changes the simulated shape.\nDr Rowan Leith: Dusk also reduces contrast as the display continues, so usable observations may end before the birds enter their roost.\nDr Rowan Leith: In the second stage of our model, a disturbance may first affect birds near an edge before moving through the group rather than starting at its centre.\nDr Rowan Leith: As the model flock deforms, edge position changes continuously, so the team tracks trajectories rather than assigning permanent edge birds.\nDr Rowan Leith: Predators may provoke compression, splitting or rapid escape, but similar shapes can appear without a confirmed attack.\nDr Rowan Leith: Roost location influences the direction from which birds arrive, but arrival flow should not be confused with the interaction rules inside a dense flock.\nDr Rowan Leith: The team calls the propagated change a turning wave: orientation shifts rapidly while relative positions remain coordinated.\nDr Rowan Leith: The camera procedure calibrates each position against fixed landmarks before paths are tracked frame by frame.\nDr Rowan Leith: Fast propagation does not mean every bird turns simultaneously, because tiny delays accumulate across the visible wave.\nDr Rowan Leith: Our Northbridge field team uses stereophotography to turn matched image coordinates into three-dimensional bird positions.\nDr Rowan Leith: Simulations explore what follows from chosen rules; they do not prove that birds perceive distance or direction in exactly the programmed way.\nDr Rowan Leith: For the field procedure, three synchronised cameras view the same event from separated positions to build a three-dimensional reconstruction.\nDr Rowan Leith: Now consider how the reconstruction is evaluated. We compare distributions of speed, spacing and turn timing, rather than asking only whether the movement looks realistic.\nDr Rowan Leith: In this tracking procedure, the software links each reconstructed position to an individual identity from one moment to the next.\nDr Rowan Leith: For our quality-control exercise, flag uncertain reconstructions instead of assuming that every three-camera match has equal precision.\nDr Rowan Leith: The reconstructed observations narrow the alternatives by comparing predicted patterns with the timing and geometry of the recorded turns.\nDr Rowan Leith: Matching the same bird across views provides a depth estimate, which a single flat image cannot supply.\nDr Rowan Leith: Two-dimensional video compresses distance, making birds at different depths appear close together unless several views are combined.\nDr Rowan Leith: Predator events are rare in our dataset, and the observations cannot manipulate risk as a laboratory task might.\nDr Rowan Leith: Each sequence records wind speed with camera conditions, preserving its environmental context.\nDr Rowan Leith: A model that fits average spacing yet fails during rapid turns may be missing how information propagates under pressure.\nDr Rowan Leith: In our comparison, flock size and wind speed are entered as separate model inputs before the movement records are compared.\nDr Rowan Leith: For the final stage, the team tests which local mechanisms remain plausible across contrasting observations rather than imposing one universal rule.\nDr Rowan Leith: A successful model should reproduce statistical relationships across several flocks, not merely imitate one memorable outline.\nDr Rowan Leith: Further studies can test alternative interaction rules against reconstructed trajectories instead of relying on visual resemblance alone.\nDr Rowan Leith: A sudden turn does not always indicate a threat; noise, obstacles or movements by other birds can produce similar visible reactions.\nDr Rowan Leith: Neighbourhood size in a model can be defined by distance or by a fixed number of nearby birds, and those alternatives generate different predictions.\nDr Rowan Leith: In a separate comparison, examine sparse and crowded groups, but do not treat that comparison as an input to the model just described.\nDr Rowan Leith: For the final analysis, the strongest explanation connects measured movement to a testable model rather than to a striking animation alone.\nDr Rowan Leith: Alignment causes headings to converge, attraction prevents the group dispersing, and short-range repulsion reduces collisions when density rises.\nDr Rowan Leith: My conclusion is methodological: treat emergence as an explanation to test, not as a label that replaces measurement at every stage of analysis.\nDr Rowan Leith: Murmuration research is strongest when reconstructed trajectories, weather records and falsifiable models converge instead of relying on visual spectacle alone.",
